UID:f4227ab4f31aa3f4@role-civil-litigation-appellate.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20261014T213940Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261014
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] FCC v. Brown
DESCRIPTION:_________________ \n_________________ \n_________________ \n__
 _______________ \n1  Cite as: 610 U. S. ____ (2026) \nPer Curiam \nSUPREME
  COURT OF THE UNITED STATES \nNo. 26A476 \nFEDERAL COMMUNICATIONS COMMISSI
 ON v. \nSHERROD BROWN\, ET AL. \nON APPLICATION FOR STAY \nNo. 26A477 \nNA
 TIONAL REPUBLICAN CONGRESSIONAL \nCOMMITTEE\, ET AL. v. SHERROD BROWN\, \n
 ET AL. \nON APPLICATION FOR STAY \n[October 14\, 2026]\n PER CURIAM. \nOn 
 March 30\, 2026\, the Media Bureau of the Federal \nCommunications Commiss
 ion (FCC) issued a “public no-\ntice” about the FCC’s lowest  unit c
 harge (LUC) require-\nments. Those requirements\, which are outlined in 47
  \nU. S. C. §315(b)(1)(A)\, concern favorable rates that broad-\ncasters 
 must charge for campaign advertisements in the\nweeks leading up to electi
 ons. The notice stated that polit-\nical candidates\, joint fundraising co
 mmittees with noncan-\ndidate members\, and political parties engaged in c
 oordi-\nnated political activity are entitled to the LUC.\nFour candidates
  for Congress filed an application for re-\nview with the FCC\, arguing th
 at only political candidates \nare entitled to the LUC and asking the full
  Commission to\nset aside the public notice as unlawful.  Two months later
 \, \nwhile their application was still pending before the Com-\nmission\, 
 the candidates filed a petition for review against \n  \n \n \n \n \n \n \
 n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n \n  \n \n2 FCC v. BROWN \nPer Curiam \nthe FC
 C and the United States in the Court of Appeals for \nthe Fourth Circuit s
 eeking the same relief.  A divided panel \nof the Fourth Circuit determine
 d that it had jurisdiction to\nconsider the candidates’ challenge\, gran
 ted their petition\,\nand set aside the public notice. In doing so\, the m
 ajority\nreasoned that the plain text and legislative history of \n§315(b
 )(1)(A) limits the availability of the LUC to political\ncandidates. \nOn 
 September 4\, 2026\, we granted an application to stay \nthe Fourth Circui
 t’s decision.  In our per curiam opinion\, we\nexplained that the Commun
 ications Act “makes clear” that\nthe \nhttps://www.supremecourt.gov/op
 inions/26pdf/26a476_k537.pdf
URL:https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/26pdf/26a476_k537.pdf
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
